d  what we've the first thing we figured  out was that mutations that activate the  Lin 14 gene are deletions not of the  open reading frame of the gene here it's  downstream of the open reading frame but  actually mutations in the three prime  untranslated region or a translocation  of the three prime untranslated region  that takes these conserved regions shown  in gray and either deletes them or or  translocates them away so that said that  there's like off switch on the link 14  gene and this is part of the off switch  and the way it gets turned off is by  this negative regulator linen for shown  here and so what was Lin for and the key  breakthrough here came out of Victor's  lab when he was a an assistant professor  at Harvard and what his lab figured out  Roslyn Lee and Rhonda Finebaum and  Victor was that Lin for didn't code for  a protein at all it codes for a very  small RNA that's 22 nucleotides long  that's shown in the gray box here and  the precursor of Lin for it's shown as  this double-stranded RNA and this RNA is  up regulated right at the time of l1  which fits with the phenotype that what  we were studying so after the Ambrose  lab figured out that Lin for codes a  tiny RNA we Victor and I compared the  sequence of his Lin 4 shown on the  bottom strand here to the sequence of  Lin 14 assuming that it might be a  direct interaction which you never know  if it's direct or indirect in biology  but let's assume simplicity and so we  could immediately see that there were  seven different sites  the lin-14 utr and so they're numbered  one through seven here and for example  here's number one and that's shown over  here there's a top strand is lin-14 the  bottom strand is lin four and in every  case there was duplex but you can notice  that it's not a perfect duplex so the  lin 4 will bulge us see here it will  bulge an AC see you see a over here and  each one had a sort of a non perfect  duplex on the other hand we could see by  evolutionary comparisons comparing the  sequence of lin 14 on the top strand to  between sin arab Dittus elegans and  briggsie that all the bolded letters are  conserved between c elegans and super  eggsy so that said that the sequence of  this is is important enough for the  animal to have conserved it over many  millions of years and so we proved that  this these elements were important by  moving this three prime UTR and the  reporter genes and knocking out these  sites to prove it was important and this  was the first example of a regulatory  RNA that was that small in the case of  Lin form and it was much smaller than  any other regulatory RNA for example the  u RNAs or more like a hundred  nucleotides than 20 nucleotides in this  if the developmental biology community  it was the community we were a part of  that he included for example many c  elegans geneticists manager softly  geneticists they paid attention to this  we definitely talked about this at  Gordon conferences and things like that  but there was always a view that this is  a kind of our quirky thing that maybe a  quirk of the lineage of C elegans  because remember we're dealing with with  a lineage here which was a distinct way  to think about developmental biology and  really a sort of marginal in comparison  to how the wider developmental biology  community thought more about tissue  interactions and cell interactions and  so yes we presented it at meetings and  things like  but it wasn't really there wasn't a  stampede for people to start to work on  that that's for sure on the other hand  the regulatory RNA community the people  who work on the ribosome and how tRNA is  bind to the acceptor sites that sort of  thing  splicing they were totally enthralled  with this and so it it was exciting to  us to sort of be thrust into the  regulatory RNA community remember we're  coming as developmental biologists what  what we happen to find a regulatory RNA  and its target thrusts us into a new  ecosystem and that ecosystem informed us  in many ways  it's very sophisticated that how the  ribosomal RNA folds how the ribosome  works these are these are inspirational  fields and it's one of the great things  about biology is that these disease  islands of specialization and you can  learn a lot from each Island and biology  teaches us that that that islands are  where the the most extreme variation  occurs so how does the Lin for micro RNA  work well it Lin for gets up regulated  at the l1 stage and that causes less  production of the lin-14 protein that's  its target but it doesn't really affect  the mRNA levels nearly as much as it  affects the protein levels so that said  that the way micro rna's work this one  micro RNA is by regulating the  translation of target proteins we still  don't know even 20 years later exactly  how that's working it's still an object  of much research in in micro rna's the  next stage right now we're at 1993  approximately the next stage is seven  years later when we start doing genetics  to try and fill out the rest of the  pathway to figure out who else is being  regulated to specify these lineages and  we get let's Evan as a suppressor of a  Weakland 14 mutation and when we figure  out what let-7 is we figure out that  it's another micro in a in this case  looks a lot like the first one is  homologous at all but it's analogous it  has the same stem-loop structure the  gray box as the 22 nucleotide product of  that and in this case the the let-7  micro RNA when we compared its sequence  across emerging databases now remember  this is the year 2000 as opposed to 1992  so genome databases are much more  sophisticated the human genome in the  year 2000 is about 30% done so we could  compare it to the 30% done genome  sequence not by doing northern blots or  anything like that but by just doing a  blast and analysis just asking is the  nucleotides conserved and boom we could  see it 20 seconds later and that was an  amazing moment because the activation  energy to actually do a northern blot or  some kind of experiment to look for  conservation there's takes some oomph to  do that but looking in a genome you  could do it in 20 seconds to know the  answer and so we sort of instantly knew  that let-7 was conserved in flies and  more in humans in genome sequences but  we also had to of course prove that it  was a small RNA so we did do a lot of  northern blots to a whole zoo of  creatures and that was very influenced  by a comparative developmental biology  conferences that Eric Davidson had run  it at marine biological labs where I'm  actually giving this speaking to this  camera right now now at about the same  time that we were finding this second  mic Rene in the year 2000 fire and  mellow and baulkham and Hamilton had  found that tiny RNAs are produced by in  RNA interference which was a very hot  topic at that point it was very  mysterious how injecting double-stranded  RNAs and de organisms would inactivate  genes and Hamilton and Baulkham found 22  nucleotide RNAs  I was enthralled by the fact that the  micro rna's and the the srna's are the  same size and I remembered the number 22  was an important number in the  Kabbalistic numerology  and sort of as a yuk I found websites to  show my lab and they they worried about  me doing this much more productive was  to say okay if if they produce it the  same size RNAs maybe they use some of  the same enzymology and the enzymes that  take double-stranded RNAs and chop them  into srna's were being discovered for  example by greg Hannan's lab at Cold  Spring Harbor and so we could do the  experiment of in activating the gene for  that double stranded RNA Dicer are an  ace Dicer and show that that was  actually important in how microRNAs get  processed as well the other importance  of the of the synchrony of the  discoveries of the RNA AI pathway and  the micro pathway is that there was a  huge interest in RNA I as a tool the C  elegans community was probably the one  of the very first genetic communities to  start using RNA I in a big way but it  also started to having a lot plants and  once si RNAs were discovered in animals  they started to be used all over animal  labs so the the interest in it is a tool  was was immense and it made the interest  in micro rna's and small arrays in  general much larger than it would have  been if it was just my current ease  so after let-7 was discovered as a as a  conserved micro neighs there was a  concerted effort to purify by cloning  many different micro rna's and actually  victor Ambrose's lab was one of the  first to do that along with the Bartel  lab and the touch' lab and many  microRNAs were discovered and within a  year or two there were thousands of  known my current a's and the current  census must be tens of thousands of  micro rna's and it is known now that  micro rna's regulate many many different  targets and are regulating those targets  to regulate all kinds of processes they  work everywhere from floral development  in plants to perhaps how memories are  formed there's thousands and thousands  of papers on micro rna's in all  different fields from plants and animal  more generally the small RNA field it's  thought that there's many sources of  double-stranded RNAs one of those  sources is micro RNAs as I started with  but there's also viral double-stranded  RNAs repeats in the genome they go  through this process of making tiny RNAs  using Dicer and various other components  and they then regulate gene expression  they regulate gene expression but by a  variety of mechanisms the ones I've told  you about so far are translational  repression and RNA degradation which si  RNAs mediate by simply causing cleavage  more mysterious is the regulation of  heterochromatin that also involves small  RNAs and how that histone modification  responds to small RNAs is a big mystery  today thank you very much  you

